  "explanation": {
    "body": "Its forecast takes this form: probability and distribution, over event-bound: at promotion events and quarterly until next rerun. It is graded on a score that rewards probabilities that land close to what actually happens. It would be ended if backcast verdict published first. Forward and quarterly lists ledgered before any rerun data for that cycle is touched; graded by the rerun's own published rule, all cells in the denominator, scored by rank correlation of predicted against realised failure order, Brier per cell, and count error per cohort. It counts as proven at next pre-registered rerun / promotion event (event-bound; backcast gate first).",
    "summary": "Never let a promotion headline travel alone again; every promoted claim carries its predicted survival, and today's top-grade claims are ranked by how likely they are to fall at the next check."
  },
  "feature_words": "Each combination is priced only from what was known on its promotion day: how comfortably it passed, its search family and time horizon, how steadily it pointed one way across stretch after stretch, how many near duplicates were promoted beside it, and whether its kind of claim had strong support in the earlier study record. One planned input was dropped and the drop is on the record: how much money could trade each combination exists for only 1,077 of the 1,217 and no floor was ever recorded, so no room-to-trade margin could be worked out for any of them, and nothing was filled in. Chances are set family by family; the promotion-day details order combinations inside a family without changing its chances.",
  "feed": "forecast_ledger",
  "forward_arm_words": "Armed for the next promotion: whenever a future search promotes a new group of combinations, each one gets a written chance of reaching the top grade at its own check, and the group gets a predicted top-grade count with a 90 percent range, all written within 24 hours of the promotion and before any check data for that group is read. If those 24 hours ever pass unwritten, the missed window is recorded as missed, never backdated. No new promotion has happened since October 27, 2026, so this arm has not fired yet.",
  "gate_passed": true,
  "gate_words": "The check the method had to pass first: pointed back at the same 1,217 combinations (a look backwards, labelled as one), its top-grade range had to contain the real 63 and its did-not-replicate range had to contain the real 564. It passed: top grade predicted 69.0, range 57.2 to 80.7, real 63; did not replicate predicted 547.5, range 519.9 to 575.2, real 564. The method, its pull toward the overall rates and its range method were all fixed in writing before this check was run once, and may not be adjusted after seeing the result. Because the method was built and checked on the same single cycle, passing is a consistency check, not proof.",
  "grading_words": "When the next check lands, all 63 stay in the count. Three scores are published: how closely the predicted order of failure matches the real order (failed combinations first, weakest first; combinations that reach the top grade again last), a score for each combination's failure chance against what actually happened, and the error in the predicted failure count. If those scores show the ranking is no better than the plain overall rates it started from, this page's claim is retired and its record stays up.",
  "idea_words": "A big search promoted 1,217 combinations, and a second search checked them: only 63 reached the top grade and 564 did not hold up at all. A promotion headline should never travel alone again. This page attaches a predicted survival to every promoted claim: how likely each one is to reach the top grade again at the next check, and how many of today's 63 top-grade combinations are expected to fall.",
